        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.083,
            0.884,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The portion of the Moon that faces the Earth is marked by the orange dashed lines and arrows. The illuminated portion of only this part of the Moon can be seen from the Earth. At positions B and H, more than half of the illuminated portion, called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "gibbous phase",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", can be seen. At positions D and F, less than half of the illuminated portion, called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "crescent phase",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", can be seen. The change in the fraction of the illuminated portion of the Moon seen from Earth causes phases of the Moon."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.233,
            0.885,
            0.363
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The phases that will be seen from the Earth at different positions of the Moon are shown in Fig. 11.5b. From A to C to E, we see the waning phase, and from E to G and back to A, we see the waxing phase. Since the rotation period of the Earth of one day is much smaller compared to the revolution period of the Moon which is nearly a month, on a given day, people on different parts of the Earth see nearly the same phase."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.364,
            0.883,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As can be seen in Fig. 11.5a, on the New Moon day, the Moon appears closest to the Sun and it appears farthest on the Full Moon day. Is this not what we also observed in Activity 11.1?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.077,
            0.423,
            0.876,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
